Found while working k211/k213 on Windows 2026-11-22 (Windows 11 Pro 26H2, go1.27.0, native run). TestMaterializeTwoRunsAtOnce (cmd/launcher/skills_test.go:1379) fails intermittently on Windows with "rename ... unreagent-.tmp-...: Access denied" (German system message "Zugriff verweigert"). unreagent-reviewer: 3 of 4 isolated runs on base 1ea8353 with -count=4; the k211 worker: 2 of 10 reruns. It passed in full-suite runs.

The test runs two materializeSkills calls at once into the same directory. On Windows a rename onto a path another process/handle has open fails, unlike POSIX rename. So this may be a real race when two launchers (or a launcher and a reader such as the agent) materialize skills into the same folder on Windows, not only a flaky test.

Owner: unreagent-worker (cmd/launcher/skills.go), unreagent-test-writer for the test if the code is right. Done when the cause is named (test vs. product), and two concurrent materializations on Windows either succeed or fail with a clear message, pinned by the test running green repeatedly (-count=20) on Windows.

Main agent 2026-11-26: maintainer asked to work the Linux-doable cards; started from Linux. Windows-native verification stays open and is labeled unverified.

2026-11-26 03:53 Cause (worker, from source; Windows unverified): product, not only the test. The failing rename is sweepSkillDirs' rename of a leftover to a fresh unreagent-.tmp-<rand> name (the only rename whose destination ends that way, matching the reported message; it was the only rename without a retry). With two runs at once, run B's sweep lists run A's live work directory and renames it while A has a file open in it (os.WriteFile), or a second sweep's RemoveAll has a directory open; Go opens files without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, and Windows refuses a directory rename with an open handle below it (ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED). POSIX renames it, so Linux never saw it. Product effect: two launchers (or a launcher plus a scanner/indexer) into one skills.dir gave a spurious 'WARN skills not fully written'; the set itself stayed whole. Fix in progress: sweep retries that rename after skillRetryPauses on renameInUseErrors (windows: ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED, ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION; empty elsewhere, build-tagged), clear message if still in use.

